course: dictionary
lesson: 6546
title: Newly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Newly» is an adverb. It means recently, or not long ago. It tells you how long ago something happened. In «a newly built school», the school was built not long ago.
explain: Look for the clue. «Newly» usually comes just before a past word such as built, painted, hatched, married or arrived. In «newly hatched chicks», the chicks have only just hatched out of their eggs. Ask: does the sentence tell me something happened recently?
explain: To check your meaning, swap in «recently». «A recently built school» still makes sense, so newly means recently. In science, «newly hatched caterpillars eat their eggshell» means the caterpillars have only just hatched.
fact: «Newly» means recently, or not long ago.
fact: «Newly» is an adverb, so it describes when or how something happened.
fact: «Newly» often comes before a past word, such as «newly built», «newly painted» or «newly hatched».
fact: «Newly» has a rare second meaning: in a new or different way.
fact: «Keep off the grass» signs mean the grass may be newly seeded or resting, so walk on the paths.
exercise: «The school welcomed newly arrived pupils this week.» What does newly mean? | *Recently, not long ago | Often | Very old | Slowly
exercise: «Newly hatched clownfish drift in the open sea.» Which word tells you the fish hatched only a short time ago? | open | drift | sea | *newly
exercise: «The children sat on the newly painted bench.» What does a newly painted bench mean? | A bench that is painted every single day | A bench that is very old and faded | *A bench that was painted not long ago | A bench that was hidden under the grass
test: Which word could replace «newly» in «a newly built school» without changing the meaning? | Slowly | Brightly | *Recently | Never
test: «A newly married couple» means a couple who | *Got married not long ago | Have never been married | Were married in a park by a river | Have been married for many years
exam: In «The treaty divided newly found lands», what does «newly» suggest about the lands? | They are lost forever | *They were found only recently, not long ago | They were found only recently | They are very large
